+# The Force Engine co-op prototype
+
+This branch adds an experimental two-player network co-op vertical slice to **The Force Engine** for the 1995 release of *Star Wars: Dark Forces*.
+
+It is an engine fork, not a normal GOB/ZIP content mod. Both players must own the game and point The Force Engine at their own installed game data. No LucasArts/Disney/GOG game files are included.
+
+## What is implemented
+
+- One host and one client over IPv4 UDP.
+- LAN or direct-IP connection on UDP port `27500` by default.
+- Build/content compatibility handshake before a client is accepted.
+- 30 Hz replication of player position, sector, view direction, weapon, health, shields, and mission-goal masks.
+- A visible remote-player ghost in the current level.
+- Reliable, acknowledged replication of player-fired projectiles.
+- Reliable, acknowledged replication of the `Use` action for doors, switches, elevators, and INF triggers.
+- Host-owned trigger/item objective masks.
+- Ping/RTT statistics, retransmission, duplicate suppression, disconnect handling, and peer timeout handling.
+- Cross-platform socket implementation for Windows and POSIX systems.
+- Standalone protocol and loopback integration tests.
+
+## Important scope limits
+
+This is a **first playable networking prototype**, not yet a campaign-complete co-op release.
+
+The following systems still require deeper engine work before all fourteen missions can be completed reliably together:
+
+- Enemy AI and enemy health/state are still simulated independently on each machine.
+- Pickups, inventory, keys, ammo, boss state, generators, scripted actors, moving geometry, and arbitrary INF state are not generally host-authoritative.
+- Level selection, briefing/cutscene playback, mission completion, and level transitions are not automatically coordinated.
+- Both players must manually start the same mission with matching data and gameplay settings.
+- Join-in-progress, co-op save files, reconnect/resume, NAT traversal, relay servers, matchmaking, anti-cheat, and native split-screen are not implemented.
+- Mods are not included in the compatibility hash yet. Test with the unmodified campaign and matching TFE settings.
+- The remote player is deliberately non-colliding and is not yet a true second `PlayerContext`; enemies do not consistently target it.
+
+These limitations are documented explicitly so this build is not mistaken for the completed campaign project.
+
+## Windows usage
+
+Place the co-op build in a normal The Force Engine folder and configure the Dark Forces source-data path as usual.
+
+Host:
+
+```bat
+theforceengine.exe --game DARK --coop-host 27500 --coop-name Host --coop-debug
+```
+
+Client on the same LAN:
+
+```bat
+theforceengine.exe --game DARK --coop-connect 192.168.1.20 27500 --coop-name Client --coop-debug
+```
+
+Replace `192.168.1.20` with the host computer's IPv4 address. Permit `theforceengine.exe` through Windows Defender Firewall for private networks. For play across the internet, forward UDP port `27500` to the host or use a trusted private-network/VPN solution. Automatic NAT traversal is not present.
+
+The included `Host-Coop.bat` and `Join-Coop.bat` launchers provide the same commands interactively.
+
+## Test procedure
+
+1. Use the same co-op executable on both computers.
+2. Use matching original game data and disable other mods.
+3. Start the host and client commands.
+4. Wait for `Connection state: connected` in `the_force_engine_log.txt`.
+5. On both installations, start the same mission and difficulty.
+6. Confirm that the remote stormtrooper-shaped ghost moves.
+7. Test primary/secondary fire and several doors, switches, and elevators.
+8. Keep `--coop-debug` enabled while reporting problems; it logs RTT, packet counts, retries, and pending reliable events.
+
+A mismatch is rejected before connection with:
+
+```text
+Co-op content/build hash differs from the host
+```
+
+## Standalone tests
+
+On Linux or another POSIX development environment:
+
+```bash
+./tests/coop/build_and_run.sh
+```
+
+The script compiles with warnings treated as errors and runs:
+
+- packet serialization/validation tests;
+- host/client loopback handshake;
+- bidirectional player-state replication;
+- reliable projectile and use events;
+- ACK removal;
+- disconnect recovery;
+- mismatched-content rejection.
+
